<Concept title="Techniques" section="Mechanics">
  <p>The key words <strong>MUST</strong>, <strong>MUST NOT</strong>, and <strong>MAY</strong> are used as in RFC 2119.</p>

  <h2>Tiers</h2>
  <p>Every technique has a <strong>tier</strong> (<code>TechniqueManager.TECHNIQUES[id].tier</code>):</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>basic</strong> — Foie, Barta, Zonde, Resta, Anti, Shifta, Deband, Jellen, Zalure. These are the <em>only</em> techniques that are learnable, assignable to the action palette, and listed in menus.</li>
    <li><strong>mid</strong> / <strong>advanced</strong> — the gi-/ra- family (Gifoie, Rafoie, Gibarta, Rabarta, Gizonde, Razonde, …) plus Grants/Megid. These have no new disks and are <strong>NOT</strong> separate selectable entries. The ra- variants are reached through the charge map; Grants/Megid have no learnable base yet and remain unavailable to fresh characters. Legacy learned advanced entries are preserved subject to class caps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Charge model</h2>
  <p>A palette technique is cast by tap or hold:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tap</strong> (released before <code>TECH_CHARGE_THRESHOLD</code>) casts the <strong>base</strong> technique.</li>
    <li><strong>Hold</strong> past the threshold casts the <strong>charged</strong> variant, resolved through <code>TechniqueManager.CHARGE_MAP</code> (e.g. <code>foie → rafoie</code>). For fresh characters the charged variant <strong>MUST</strong> be reached this way.</li>
    <li>A mid-charge technique <strong>MUST</strong> be cancelled if the avatar is hit (see <a href={siteUrl('/states/player-state')}>Player State</a>).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="ui-listing">UI listing (normative)</h2>
  <p>The action palette picker and the Start-menu <em>Techniques</em> screen <strong>MUST</strong> list <strong>only</strong> <code>tier == "basic"</code> techniques. The gi-/ra- variants <strong>MUST NOT</strong> appear as their own rows — they are an effect of charging a base tech, not a thing you select or learn. A screen that wants to surface the charge relationship <strong>MAY</strong> show it as a base → charged pairing in the <em>detail</em> view (PSO's original Techniques screen drew the base tech, a charge arrow, then the charged result), but the selectable list stays base-only.</p>
  <p><em>This is the contract behind hiding gi-/ra- from the Start menu: the list was looping all of <code>TECHNIQUES</code> unfiltered; it now filters to base tier, matching the palette.</em></p>

  <p>Disk availability, class caps and saved-level compatibility follow <a href={siteUrl('/states/technique-progression')}>Technique progression integrity</a>.</p>

  <h2>Player Barta reach</h2>
  <p>Base Barta travels at 20 units/second with a maximum projectile travel distance of 9 units, tuned from the Force Coliseum playtest. Its visible ice follows that path and may dissolve briefly after the damaging front passes. Lingering ice MUST NOT deal additional damage. Enemy-authored technique ranges and charged Rabarta are separate.</p>

  <h2>Resta and Anti party delivery</h2>
  <p>Resta and Anti affect the caster and living local party recipients within 6 units in 3D at release. Resta retains its learned-level healing amount, capped at each recipient’s maximum HP. Anti clears the recipient’s current ailments. Neither spell revives defeated allies or affects enemies. Range is a radius check without a line-of-sight gate. A single cast consumes PP once and plays one positional sound; a brief ground ring marks the radius. Success particles attach only to recipients whose HP or ailments actually changed, then expire. Full-health and already-clean allies MUST NOT display success particles.</p>
  <p>Companion HP and curable ailments are runtime state. This delivery does not establish enemy targeting of companions, companion ailment combat behavior, network replication, or persistent companion health across scene changes.</p>

  <h2>Shifta</h2>
  <p>Shifta uses the same 6-unit living-party radius, with a red cast circle. The runtime buff raises the attack stat by 10% per learned level (the existing Shifta strength formula) for a provisional 60 seconds. Recasting refreshes the timer and retains the stronger amount without stacking. Legacy turn-based and runtime Shifta use the stronger amount rather than multiplying twice. Red glowing clouds follow both wrist bones while the buff is active; expiry, defeat, dismissal or removal of the recipient clears the indicator and runtime bonus. The buff is scene-local and is not saved.</p>

  <h2>Deband, Jellen and Zalure</h2>
  <p>These use Shifta’s 6-unit cast radius and provisional 60-second refreshable duration. Deband raises defense for living self and party recipients. Jellen reduces enemy attack; Zalure reduces enemy defense. Enemy reductions are capped at 90%. Recasts retain the stronger amount without stacking. Attack circles and clouds are red; defense circles and clouds are blue. Wrist bones are used where available; creatures without arm bones display paired side indicators. Effects expire with their runtime stat changes. Companion defense is exposed in runtime stats; enemy targeting of companions remains outside this delivery.</p>

  <h2>Scope</h2>
  <p>PP costs, elements and status procs, and level scaling per learned disk. Disks learned from the item shop unlock <em>basic</em> techniques only.</p>

  <h2>Implemented by</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>scripts/3d/player/player.gd</code> (Player) — the hold-to-charge lifecycle (<code>_on_palette_pressed</code> / <code>_on_palette_released</code>, the per-frame charge timer in <code>_physics_process</code> crossing <code>TECH_CHARGE_THRESHOLD</code>); <code>_cast_technique</code> checks learned level + PP cost, deducts PP, and enters the cast; <code>_spawn_technique_effect</code> dispatches to the per-spell spawners (<code>_spawn_foie</code>, <code>_spawn_gifoie</code>, <code>_spawn_rafoie</code>, <code>_spawn_barta</code>, <code>_spawn_gibarta</code>, <code>_spawn_rabarta</code>, <code>_spawn_zonde</code>, <code>_spawn_gizonde</code>, <code>_spawn_razonde</code>).</li>
    <li><code>scripts/autoloads/technique_manager.gd</code> (TechniqueManager) — the technique table (<code>TECHNIQUES</code>), learned-level lookup (<code>get_technique_level</code>), and the tap-vs-charged mapping (<code>get_charged_technique</code> / <code>get_base_technique</code>) plus disk learning used by the shop.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A technique cast runs as the ATTACKING state of the <a href={siteUrl('/states/player-state')}>Player State Machine</a>; a mid-charge technique is cancelled if the avatar is hit.</p>

  <p><a href={siteUrl('/mechanics')}>← Mechanics</a></p>
</Concept>
